    "evidence_text": "RNase H and Postreplication Repair Protect Cells from Ribonucleotides Incorporated in DNA\n\nMisincorporation is limited by the selectivity of DNA replicases.\n\nMoreover, cells lacking RNase H accumulate mono- and polyubiquitylated PCNA and have a constitutively activated PRR.\n\nA Protein Inventory of Human Ribosome Biogenesis Reveals an Essential Function of Exportin 5 in 60S Subunit Export\n\nTo date, only few human homologs of these yeast ribosome synthesis factors have been characterized.\n\nThe screen was based on visual readouts, using inducible, fluorescent ribosomal proteins as reporters.\n\nRibosome Profiling Provides Evidence that Large Noncoding RNAs Do Not Encode Proteins\n\nLarge noncoding RNAs are emerging as an important component in cellular regulation.\n\nConsiderable evidence indicates that these transcripts act directly as functional RNAs rather than through an encoded protein product.\n\nInstead, we define a metric based on the known property of translation whereby translating ribosomes are released upon encountering a bona fide stop codon.\n\nWe show that this metric accurately discriminates between protein-coding transcripts and all classes of known noncoding transcripts, including lincRNAs.\n\nTaken together, these results argue that the large majority of lincRNAs do not function through encoded proteins.\n\nmiRNA-dependent gene silencing involving Ago2-mediated cleavage of a circular antisense RNA.\n\nHere, we report that miRNAs can also regulate gene expression by targeting non-coding antisense transcripts in human cells.\n\nDecoding Mammalian Ribosome-mRNA States by Translational GTPase Complexes\n\nComparative analyses reveal that each decoding factor exploits the plasticity of the ribosomal decoding center to differentially remodel ribosomal proteins and rRNA.\n\nLincRNA-p21 suppresses target mRNA translation.\n\nMammalian long intergenic noncoding RNAs (lincRNAs) are best known for modulating transcription.\n\nWe propose that HuR controls translation of a subset of target mRNAs by influencing lincRNA-p21 levels.\n\nOur findings uncover a role for lincRNA as a posttranscriptional inhibitor of translation.\n\nExpression of Versican 3\u2032-Untranslated Region Modulates Endogenous MicroRNA Functions\n\nBACKGROUND Mature microRNAs (miRNAs) are single-stranded RNAs that regulate post-transcriptional gene expression.\n\nHowever, the impact of this non-coding fragment on tumorigenesis is yet to be determined.   \n\n\nThe decrease in miRNA activity consequently translated into differences in tumor growth.\n\nCONCLUSION Our results demonstrated that a 3'UTR fragment can be used to modulate miRNA functions.\n\nThis non-coding component of mRNA may be used retrospectively to modulate miRNA activities.\n\nA cluster of ribosome synthesis factors regulate pre-rRNA folding and 5.8S rRNA maturation by the Rat1 exonuclease\n\nUV crosslinking revealed multiple Rat1-binding sites across the pre-rRNA, consistent with its known functions.\n\nITS2 was reported to undergo structural remodelling.\n\nIn vivo chemical probing indicates that A(3)-cluster binding is required for this reorganization, potentially regulating the timing of processing.\n\nChaperoning 5S RNA assembly.\n\nIn mammals, the 5S RNP is also a central regulator of the homeostasis of the tumor suppressor p53.\n\nThe complexity of human ribosome biogenesis revealed by systematic nucleolar screening of Pre-rRNA processing factors.\n\nMature ribosomal RNAs (rRNAs) are produced from polycistronic precursors following complex processing.\n\nPrecursor (pre)-rRNA processing has been extensively characterized in yeast and was assumed to be conserved in humans.",
